# Rank 1146 / CVE-2025-53106: Graylog's token-creation endpoint checks whether
# the requesting user may create tokens for themselves, then uses that answer
# to authorize creating a token for whichever other user the URL names
# (CWE-285).
#
# The handler loads the token's future owner from the {userId} path parameter
# but evaluates the instance-scoped permission against the current principal:
# RestResource.isPermitted(permission, instanceId) answers "may the subject act
# on instanceId's namespace", and the vulnerable form passes
# currentUser.getName() there while the token is minted for futureOwner.
# Pool commit 9215b8f1fd32 is the real fix (COMMIT_OK, its changelog entry
# names the advisory GHSA-3m86-c9x3-vwm9): it deletes the currentUser lookup
# entirely and checks isPermitted(USERS_TOKENCREATE, futureOwner.getName()).
# No pool correction.
#
# ATTENTION, vocabulary gap, confirmed on recheck. Nearest IDOR concepts
# describe a missing ownership constraint, not a permission check present but
# evaluated against the wrong principal, and Java has no binding for them;
# both snippets reject, and no rule or flag fires on the real handler at
# either revision (full findings and flags views, 3 x --cache off each).
#
# The wrong subject is only readable through the two locals' spellings: the
# vulnerable call's argument token is call_arg:isPermitted:currentUser.getName
# and the fixed one is call_arg:isPermitted:futureOwner.getName, so every
# discriminating route keys the application's own variable name. Relaxing to
# the spelling-free facts over-fires: a function-scope "some call receives a
# getCurrentUser-derived argument" probe reports 63 flags on the rest subtree
# alone against 1 true finding, because passing the current user as the
# owner/creator argument of a create call is correct code everywhere else in
# this codebase; and the current-principal accessor itself is the application
# helper getCurrentUser(), not library vocabulary.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-vocabulary-gap, both revisions rejected.

test "rank1146: the permission check runs against the requester, not the token's future owner"
  lang java
  file UsersResource.java
  reject_review code.IdorPolicyReview
  code
  ```
  package org.graylog2.rest.resources.users;

  public class UsersResource extends RestResource {
      public Token generateNewToken(@PathParam("userId") String userId, @PathParam("name") String name, GenerateTokenTTL body) {
          final User futureOwner = loadUserById(userId);
          final User currentUser = getCurrentUser();

          if (currentUser == null) {
              throw new ForbiddenException("Not allowed to create tokens for unknown user.");
          }
          if (!isPermitted(USERS_TOKENCREATE, currentUser.getName())) {
              throw new ForbiddenException(currentUser.getName() + " is not allowed to create token.");
          }

          final AccessToken accessToken = accessTokenService.create(futureOwner.getName(), name, body.getTTL(() -> defaultTTL()));
          return Token.create(accessToken.getId(), accessToken.getName(), accessToken.getToken(), accessToken.getLastAccess());
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1146: the permission check runs against the token's actual future owner"
  lang java
  file UsersResource.java
  reject_review code.IdorPolicyReview
  code
  ```
  package org.graylog2.rest.resources.users;

  public class UsersResource extends RestResource {
      public Token generateNewToken(@PathParam("userId") String userId, @PathParam("name") String name, GenerateTokenTTL body) {
          final User futureOwner = loadUserById(userId);

          if (!isPermitted(USERS_TOKENCREATE, futureOwner.getName())) {
              throw new ForbiddenException("You are not allowed to create a token for user " + futureOwner.getName() + ".");
          }

          final AccessToken accessToken = accessTokenService.create(futureOwner.getName(), name, body.getTTL(() -> defaultTTL()));
          return Token.create(accessToken.getId(), accessToken.getName(), accessToken.getToken(), accessToken.getLastAccess());
      }
  }
  ```
